  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 140 AM EDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nUpper-level troughing will remain anchored across the eastern CONUS \nand western Atlantic through much of the week, while surface high \npressure builds across the Southeast US. Meanwhile, a strong low-\npressure system is expected to meander off the Eastern Seaboard. The \nresulting tight pressure gradient will support breezy and gusty \nnorth-northeasterly winds across South Florida through mid-week. \nMost global guidance suites suggest this pressure gradient will \nbegin to relax towards the latter half of the week as the offshore \nlow-pressure system gradually lifts away, though persistent north-\neasterly flow will remain established across South Florida.\n\nIn terms of moisture, PWAT values are forecast to dip into the 1.1-\n1.3 inch range through mid-week as dry air continues to filter into \nthe region. Moisture values will gradually recover toward more \nseasonal levels of 1.4-1.5 inches by late week. With low-level \nconvergence maximized along the Atlantic coast, isolated to \nscattered showers and a few embedded thunderstorms could develop \neach day, favoring the East Coast coastal zones, while the rest of \nSouth Florida should expect mostly dry conditions through mid-week. \n\nThe primary hazards through most of the week will remain marine and \ncoastal concerns along the Atlantic beaches, including a high risk \nof rip currents as well as rough surf that could prompt beach \nerosion. Heading into Friday, model guidance hints at a mid/upper-\nlevel disturbance digging southward toward the region, which could \nsupport an uptick in convective activity and coverage across the \neastern half of South Florida heading into the weekend. \n\nTemperatures will trend slightly below climatological averages \ntoward the end of the week. Daytime highs will generally start in \nthe mid to upper 80s, cooling slightly into the low to mid 80s by \nlate week. Overnight lows will range from the lower 70s along the \nEast Coast to the mid to upper 60s across the interior and SW \nFlorida.",
